---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="03-loops"
  lessonSlug="00-example-countdown"
  hook="The textbook's first while-loop example, in 4 lines. The three-step flow of execution that every loop in CSC 141 follows."
>
  <h2>The function</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def countdown(n):
    while n > 0:
        print(n)
        n = n - 1
    print("Blastoff!")

countdown(3)`}
  />

  <Callout tone="note" title="Source: Think Python §7.3">
    <p>
      <em>"While n is greater than 0, display the value of n and then reduce
      the value of n by 1. When you get to 0, display the word Blastoff!"</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The three-step flow</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Evaluate the condition (<code>n &gt; 0</code>).</li>
    <li>If <strong>False</strong> → exit, continue after the loop.</li>
    <li>If <strong>True</strong> → run the body, then <strong>go back to step 1</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What makes it terminate</h2>
  <p>
    The line <code>n = n - 1</code> (same as <code>n -= 1</code>) is the only
    reason this loop ever ends. Delete it and you have an
    <strong>infinite loop</strong>:
  </p>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="⚠ Don't actually run this — it will hang the browser. Trace it by hand."
    code={`def countdown_INFINITE(n):
    while n > 0:
        print(n)
        # n never changes — condition stays True forever
    print("Blastoff!")

# countdown_INFINITE(3)   # ← would loop forever
print("(skipped — would hang)")`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="Joke from the textbook">
    <p>
      <em>"An endless source of amusement for computer scientists is the
      observation that the directions on shampoo, 'Lather, rinse, repeat,'
      are an infinite loop."</em>
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The zero-iteration edge case</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    caption="What does countdown(0) do?"
    code={`def countdown(n):
    while n > 0:
        print(n)
        n -= 1
    print("Blastoff!")

countdown(0)
# Condition is False on the FIRST check.
# Body runs zero times. Only "Blastoff!" prints.`}
  />

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <p>
    Sum 1..n uses the same shape — initialize before, update inside — but with
    a <code>for</code> loop instead of <code>while</code>.
  </p>
</Lecture>
